1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,226
What stops one admin from switching on a fraudulent shop?

2
00:00:03,226 --> 00:00:07,273
If one person could do it, one mistake or one bribe would be enough.

3
00:00:07,273 --> 00:00:14,000
So activation is a request: one admin, the maker, asks, and nothing changes yet.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:21,738
There is no single-admin activate call: one call opens a request, and others list, approve, and reject requests.

5
00:00:21,738 --> 00:00:27,595
A different admin, the checker, approves it, and only then does the merchant become active.

6
00:00:27,595 --> 00:00:34,178
A request is decided once: it must still be pending, not expired, and the checker can't be the maker.

7
00:00:34,178 --> 00:00:38,250
Self-approval answers four oh nine, with its own error code.

8
00:00:38,250 --> 00:00:44,226
Both opening and approving a request check again that every required company document is verified.

9
00:00:44,226 --> 00:00:48,440
The checker's name goes into the status history, with the maker in the reason.

10
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:52,488
Requests expire after a configured time, twenty-four hours here.

11
00:00:52,488 --> 00:00:59,321
A scheduled sweep marks overdue requests as expired, and ShedLock makes sure only one instance runs it.

12
00:00:59,321 --> 00:01:06,119
Only one pending request per merchant can exist, and a partial unique index in the database enforces it.

13
00:01:06,119 --> 00:01:11,928
Next, real logins with Keycloak, so every admin action carries a verified name.

